  ✓ CONFIRMED: Router balance sweeping allows liquidity withdrawers to stea
    Justification: Upon reviewing Router.removeLiquidityExact, 
Router.removeLiquiditySingle, and Router.swapAssetToSynth, I confirmed an 
exploitable balance-sweeping vulnerability. In removeLiquidityExact for the BNB 
pool, Pool.remove() sends BASE and WBNB to the Router, after which the Router 
reads its entire BASE and WBNB balances and transfers or unwraps them, without 
recording balances before the operation. More significantly, 
removeLiquiditySingle always calls Pool.remove() to send both assets to the 
Router; when toBase is true, it transfers only the token balance back to the 
pool, leaving the removed BASE stranded in the Router. A subsequent user with 
any valid LP position can invoke the opposite withdrawal path, which transfers 
the Router’s entire BASE balance into the pool and ultimately sends the 
resulting asset to that user. The same issue exists in swapAssetToSynth, where 
the Router forwards its complete BASE balance after sellTo, allowing 
pre-existing BASE to fund another user’s synth mint. No access control or 
balance-delta accounting prevents these transfers.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Curated-pool dividend payouts are unlimited despite maxTrade
    Justification: Upon reviewing Router.sol functions getsDividend, 
addTradeFee, and addDividend, I confirmed that the configured maxTrades value is
not enforced as a per-era event limit or cumulative allocation cap. For every 
qualifying curated-pool swap, getsDividend calls addTradeFee and then 
addDividend, while addDividend calculates dailyAllocation as the current Reserve
balance divided by eraLength and maxTrades, and immediately calls 
Reserve.grantFunds. No trade counter, era identifier, reset mechanism, or 
aggregate dividend tracking exists. Consequently, after the fee-average window 
is populated and emissions are enabled, an attacker can repeatedly execute swaps
against a curated pool and trigger additional grants beyond maxTrades events in 
the same era. Reserve.grantFunds only limits each transfer to the reserve's 
current balance and does not impose a Router-specific budget; although the 
reserve balance declines, repeated calls can cumulatively distribute 
substantially more than the intended era allocation and can continue until the 
reserve is depleted. The curated-pool and nonzero-average checks are eligibility
conditions, not rate limits.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Router ignores token transfer return values and received amo
    Justification: Upon reviewing the Router transfer paths and 
PoolFactory.createPoolADD, I confirmed an exploitable unsafe-token interaction 
vulnerability. Router._handleTransferIn computes a balance delta for ERC20 
inputs but does not require transferFrom to return true, and 
addLiquidityForMember, buyTo, sellTo, and related callers ignore the returned 
actual amount and continue using the requested amount in subsequent Pool 
operations. Router._handleTransferOut similarly invokes ERC20 transfer without 
checking its return value; therefore, a token that returns false can cause a 
swap or liquidity-removal operation to complete while the user receives no 
payout, leaving assets stranded in the Router. The WBNB transfer in 
_handleTransferIn is also unchecked. PoolFactory._handleTransferIn has the same 
unchecked transferFrom pattern during createPoolADD. Although some failed 
transfers may cause downstream Pool logic to revert depending on its balance 
checks, no general guard prevents silent false-return transfers or 
fee-on-transfer discrepancies, and balance-delta results are not propagated. A 
listed or newly created token with non-standard or fee-on-transfer behavior can 
therefore produce incorrect accounting or silent payout failures.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: BondVault claim logic can permanently lock half of a member’
    Justification: Upon reviewing BondVault.sol, I confirmed the logic error in 
claimForMember and calcBondedLP. calcBondedLP computes the unlocked amount using
elapsed time and claimRate, and claimForMember then updates lastBlockTime and 
subtracts _claimable from bondedLP before executing the final-claim test. The 
condition at the relevant code in claimForMember, `if (_claimable == 
mapBondAsset_memberDetails.bondedLP)`, compares the claim against the post-claim
remainder rather than the pre-claim balance or zero. Consequently, when exactly 
half of a bond is claimable, the subtraction leaves the other half in bondedLP, 
making the equality true and setting claimRate to zero. Subsequent calls to 
calcBondedLP therefore return zero despite the positive bondedLP balance, so the
remaining LP cannot vest or be claimed through normal claim functions. The path 
is reachable because Dao.claimForMember and claimAllForMember invoke the vault, 
and depositForMember can also trigger claimForMember. No guard corrects the rate
afterward; only a new deposit or release can potentially change the state.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Integer truncation can create permanently unclaimable BondVa
    Justification: Upon reviewing BondVault.depositForMember, calcBondedLP, and 
claimForMember in contracts/BondVault.sol, I confirmed the funds-lockup 
condition. depositForMember calculates claimRate as bondedLP divided by 
bondingPeriodSeconds using integer division, with no remainder accounting. 
Consequently, any deposit whose LP amount is smaller than the configured vesting
period (15,552,000 seconds by default) receives a zero claimRate. calcBondedLP 
then computes elapsed time multiplied by this zero rate and returns zero unless 
the privileged bondRelease flag is enabled, so the member cannot obtain the 
bonded LPs through Dao.claimForMember or claimAllForMember. The same issue can 
affect residual amounts only where a recalculation produces a zero rate; 
ordinary nonzero-rate residuals eventually become claimable because calcBondedLP
caps claims at the remaining balance. The only apparent escape is 
BondVault.release(), restricted to the DAO or deployer and not exposed through a
normal user path. Thus, the vulnerable zero-rate case is exploitable as a user 
funds lockup, although an authorized administrative release may recover all 
bonds globally.
  ✗ REJECTED: BondVault ignores LP transfer failure during claims
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged unchecked external call in 
BondVault.claimForMember, I determined this is a false positive for the deployed
architecture. The function does discard the boolean returned by 
iBEP20(_pool).transfer(member, _claimable) after updating accounting and 
invoking decreaseWeight, but _pool is obtained from PoolFactory.getPool(asset), 
whose mappings are populated only with contracts deployed directly via new 
Pool(...). Pool.transfer() unconditionally calls _transfer() and returns true on
success; it does not contain a false-return path. If the vault lacks sufficient 
LP balance, _transfer() reverts, and Solidity transaction atomicity rolls back 
the prior timestamp, bondedLP, claim-rate, and weight updates. The underlying 
asset token’s transfer behavior is irrelevant because claims transfer the Pool’s
LP token, not the underlying token. Although checking the return value would be 
defensive best practice, the supplied code does not provide a practical route 
for a non-compliant token or false return from the resolved pool. A different 
factory implementation or upgradeable pool design could change this conclusion, 
but neither is shown here.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Harvested Synth rewards bypass the minimum withdrawal lock
    Justification: Upon reviewing the SynthVault deposit, harvestSingle, 
calcCurrentReward, and _processWithdraw functions, I confirmed that the alleged 
lock-bypass vulnerability is exploitable. _deposit updates both 
mapMemberSynth_lastTime and the member-wide mapMember_depositTime to 
block.timestamp + minimumDepositTime. However, harvestSingle only refreshes 
mapMemberSynth_lastTime before adding synthReward to mapMemberSynth_deposit; it 
does not update mapMember_depositTime. Once the original deposit lock expires, a
member can call harvestSingle, which is permitted because calcCurrentReward 
checks only the synth-specific timestamp, and the newly minted reward is 
immediately added to the withdrawable balance. The member can then call 
withdraw, whose _processWithdraw function checks only block.timestamp > 
mapMember_depositTime[_member], allowing withdrawal of a percentage of the 
combined principal and harvested rewards without a new lock period. No later 
check uses the refreshed synth-specific timestamp to restrict withdrawal, and 
harvestAll has the same underlying behavior because it delegates to 
harvestSingle. Therefore, the implementation does not enforce the intended 
withdrawal lock on harvested rewards.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: SynthVault harvestAll becomes increasingly gas-heavy and can
    Justification: Upon reviewing SynthVault.harvestAll and _deposit, I 
confirmed the reported scalability-related denial-of-service condition. 
harvestAll iterates from index zero through the entire stakedSynthAssets array 
on every call, without pagination, a caller-supplied range, or any 
membership-based index. _deposit permanently appends each previously unseen 
synth to this array via isStakedSynth and push, so the loop domain is 
monotonically increasing and is never pruned. Although non-member entries 
generally avoid the more expensive harvestSingle path because their calculated 
reward is zero, every entry still incurs storage reads, timestamp checks, and 
reward calculation, while entries with an active balance and reward can trigger 
multiple external calls through the reserve, pool factory, pool, utilities, and 
synth contracts. A user can expand the global array by depositing across many 
valid synths, subject to synth and pool curation. No guard limits the array size
or bounds gas usage. harvestSingle remains an alternative for individual assets,
so this does not necessarily disable all harvesting, but harvestAll can 
eventually exceed the block gas limit and become unusable as historical assets 
accumulate.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Withdrawal basis points are not range-validated, enabling ca
    Justification: Upon reviewing SynthVault.withdraw and _processWithdraw, I 
confirmed that the stated validation and accounting weaknesses exist. withdraw 
forwards the caller-supplied basisPoints without enforcing a maximum of 10,000, 
while _processWithdraw independently invokes Utils.calcPart for the stored 
deposit and synth weight. With integer, floor-based proportional arithmetic, a 
partial withdrawal can therefore reduce mapMemberSynth_deposit while reducing 
mapMemberSynth_weight by zero when the weight is below the rounding threshold. 
The member can repeat such withdrawals and retain voting or reward weight after 
removing the corresponding synth principal, potentially harvesting rewards 
against stale weight before a later full withdrawal clears it. The global and 
per-member weight totals are updated using the independently rounded weight 
value, so no invariant repairs this divergence. A basisPoints value above 10,000
will generally cause subtraction underflow and revert under Solidity 0.8.3; this
primarily makes the caller’s own transaction fail rather than harming other 
users, but it still demonstrates missing input validation. The lockout only 
restricts timing and does not prevent either issue.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Arbitrary zero-value deposits can repeatedly lock another us
    Justification: Upon reviewing the SynthVault deposit flow, I confirmed that 
the alleged denial-of-service vulnerability is exploitable. In depositForMember,
the contract validates only that the synth is recognized and that transferFrom 
succeeds; it does not require amount to be greater than zero or restrict the 
member address. A caller can therefore invoke depositForMember(validSynth, 
victim, 0), subject to the token accepting a zero-value transferFrom. The 
subsequent _deposit call unconditionally sets 
mapMemberSynth_lastTime[_member][_synth] and mapMember_depositTime[_member] to 
block.timestamp + minimumDepositTime, even when _amount is zero. It also records
the member as a synth member, although no deposit balance or weight is added. 
These timestamps directly gate calcCurrentReward/harvest eligibility and 
_processWithdraw, so repeated zero-value calls can continually extend the 
victim's withdrawal and harvest lockouts without adding assets. No 
authorization, consent, existing-membership, or positive-amount check mitigates 
this path. The impact depends on the synth token's zero-transfer behavior, but 
standard BEP-20 implementations generally permit zero transfers, making the 
attack path clear.


